y of a sentence was  about 15 months it's now about 30 months  so you can understand why you have a  prison population that is so much larger  there even though  crime rate is about what it was in 1972  that's because we sent a lot more people  to prison and we make them stay there  longer if you had to pick the specific  battle to fight that you think would  have the biggest positive impact on the  kind of status quo of incarceration in  the US is it where at where would you  attack it if you could only focus on one  area it'd be a tough call between  changing sentencing for people convicted  of drug crimes and eliminating mandatory  minimum sentences both of those have  contributed their own enormous piece to  the math problem mass incarceration but  and they're related because some of the  mandatory minimum sentences are from  drug crimes but they are targeting a  slide if enough different group that you  could do something about if you said  nobody can go to prison for possession  of drugs you would have an impact on  mass incarceration but it would be  smaller than you would think because  people who go to prison for possession  of drugs tend to stay there not very  long a mandatory minimum sentences on  the other hand are quite significant  because not only are you not being  placed on probation but you're also  typically being sensed too much longer  than the judge would ordinarily impose  so mandatory minimums in the federal  system for example or 10 20 and 30 years  and if you eliminated those and allowed  judicial discretion for choosing  sentences lower than those mandatory  minimums you would have a significant  impact so be tough call between those  two I take either one of those if you  force my hand i would say that there is  a mandatory minimum sentence to prison  is not allowed and if that were always  the case that a person could make that  you could make a case for a  community-based penalty for any person  convicted of any crime i think or a  lesser sentence than well then what the  law provides is a maximum for any crime  I think you'd have more impact on mass  incarceration jovi if we went back to  the sentencing laws of 1980 within a  very few years we would have the prison  population of 1980 if we put aside just  for a second the the disproportionate  application of on minorities  when we look at these mandatory minimums  drug crimes we look at higher drug usage  rates among whites but higher  incarceration rates for drug usage among  minorities putting that aside just  briefly let's imagine that weren't the  problem I get emails from people on both  sides of the political spectrum saying  listen we understand all of the the this  this minority disproportionate stuff but  when you put people in prison they  aren't committing crimes so it is still  better for the surrounding community is  that even true can we even still can we  even say that accurately yeah and the  answer that is increasingly it's  becoming clear that that is not a not a  meaningful argument used to be that it's  a penance and you know makes sense on  the face you lock a person up that  person can't commit crimes but there are  two mechanisms that really reduce the  that's usually called the incapacitation  effect of a prison sentence one is  replacement most crimes that are  committed in communities are committed  by young men and groups and if you  incarcerate one person from that group  you may have no impact on the group's  level of criminal activity in other  words the number of crimes that actually  get committed and what you may do  actually is open up a place for someone  else to join in who might not have been  involved in that criminal network now  become involved this is particularly  true drug-related crimes you don't when  you lock up a person for selling drugs  you don't prevent a single drug from  being sold you just create a job opening  for somebody else to sell drugs and when  you do this in a framework where there  