 economic activity to make sure that  homes are taken care of to buy the  things that kids need to go to school on  and on and on we also know that having a  parent go to prison reduces your chances  of being involved as a child reduces  your chances of being involved on the  juvenile justice system I'm sorry I mean  to say that again we know that going to  prison increases the chances of a child  going to the juvenile justice system by  one-fourth so when you have a community  in which every child is connected to an  adult who's been to prison you have a  community in which all those children  are more vulnerable to to the juvenile  justice system being arrested we also  know that that increases the chance of a  physical health problems we know that  increases the chance of emotional  problems we know for example that in  neighborhoods where lots of people are  cycling in and out of the prison system  sexually transmitted diseases are much  better much higher rate because in  intimate relationships between men and  women have been destabilized so if you  add all these things up together you get  a situation in which incarceration  policies which have dramatically  increased the number of people going to  prison from  inner-city neighborhoods have the impact  of making those neighborhoods less  stable and and and increasing the level  of the contribute contributors to  criminality in those neighborhoods  that's why you can have nationally a  drop in crime rate but in some  neighborhoods the crime rate stays very  stubbornly high and it's partly because  incarceration has made it hard for those  neighborhoods to develop the kind of  stable social patterns that really  produce Public Safety yes and not only  do we have to look at the kind of  cultural and punitive and rehabilitative  aspects of these prison sentences but  their significant economic aspects as  well over overtime to these  neighborhoods we've been speaking with  Todd clear he's the Dean of the School  